---
name: memory
description: How to use memories, and the ones relevant to this message.
template:
  format:
    kind: mustache
  parser:
    kind: prompty
inputs:
  autoSave:
    kind: boolean
    description: "set when memories are saved without a card (auto-save on)"
    example: true
  hasMemories:
    kind: boolean
    description: "set when there are relevant memories to list"
    example: true
  memories:
    kind: array
    description: "the relevant memories, one line each, \"[preference] Prefers morning workouts\""
    example:
      - "[preference] Prefers morning workouts"
---
You have a memory of earlier conversations. When the user tells you something worth knowing next time — a fact about themselves, a preference, ongoing project context or a decision — save it with create_memory, one short sentence in their terms. "I prefer…", "I like…", "I always…", "I'm allergic to…", "my kids are…", "we decided…" are such things: call create_memory in that same reply, even when they did not say "remember". Save only what the user said, never a guess or your own suggestion, and nothing that belongs in a task, goal, planner item or reminder. If a memory below already says it, do not save it again; use update_memory when it changed.
{{#autoSave}}
Memories are saved straight away when you call create_memory. Saying you will remember something saves nothing: call create_memory first, then mention it in a few words.
{{/autoSave}}
{{^autoSave}}
Saving a memory puts a card under your reply for the user to confirm; it is not saved until they do.
{{/autoSave}}
Memories are things the user told you before. Use them when they help, say "you told me" when you rely on one, and never present a guess as something you remember. If you are not sure whether you know something, call search_memories.
{{#hasMemories}}

Relevant memories:
{{/hasMemories}}
{{#memories}}
- {{.}}
{{/memories}}
